---
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import schema from '../../generated/schema.json';
import { dailyRun, dataFlow, deployment, valueActivity } from '../../lib/pipeline-diagrams';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';

const count = (layer: string) => schema.layers.find((l) => l.name === layer)?.models.length ?? 0;
const checks = schema.checks as { name: string; severity: string }[];
const errorChecks = checks.filter((c) => c.severity === 'error');
const warnChecks = checks.filter((c) => c.severity !== 'error');
const f = schema.fantasy as {
  fantasy: Record<string, { teams: number; roster_spots: number; prior_games: number; durability_games: number; minutes_recency: number[] }>;
  fantasy_recency: number[];
  fantasy_durability: { typical_share: number };
  fantasy_team_change: number;
  fantasy_recent_minutes: number;
  fantasy_matchup: { prior_games: number };
};
const nba = f.fantasy['nba']!;
const wnba = f.fantasy['wnba']!;
const m = (name: string) => href(`/schema/#m-${name}`);

const [dfd, deploy, activity] = await Promise.all([dataFlow(), deployment(), valueActivity()]);
const sequence = dailyRun();
const link = 'underline underline-offset-4';
---

<Base
  title="Pipeline"
  description="How data moves from the league and ESPN APIs to this site: a data-flow diagram, a deployment diagram, the sequence of a daily run, how board value is computed, and every stage in detail."
>
  <section>
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Pipeline</h1>
    <p class="mt-1 max-w-3xl text-sm text-muted">
      End to end, from two public APIs to this site. Raw responses land unchanged; dbt models them on Databricks through
      bronze, silver and gold; a quality gate decides whether the night's build is published; the published build in
      Cloudflare R2 is what every page, notebook and dashboard reads. The diagrams are drawn when the site is built, and
      every model they name is on the <a href={href('/schema/')} class={link}>Schema</a> page.
    </p>

    <nav class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-sm" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#data-flow" class="rounded-full border border-line px-3 py-1 hover:bg-card">Data flow</a>
      <a href="#deployment" class="rounded-full border border-line px-3 py-1 hover:bg-card">Deployment</a>
      <a href="#daily-run" class="rounded-full border border-line px-3 py-1 hover:bg-card">A daily run</a>
      <a href="#value" class="rounded-full border border-line px-3 py-1 hover:bg-card">Board value</a>
      <a href="#stages" class="rounded-full border border-line px-3 py-1 hover:bg-card">Stages</a>
      <a href="#workflows" class="rounded-full border border-line px-3 py-1 hover:bg-card">Other workflows</a>
      <a href="#gaps" class="rounded-full border border-line px-3 py-1 hover:bg-card">Gaps closed</a>
    </nav>

    <section id="data-flow" class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Data flow</h2>
      <Diagram
        id="dfd"
        svg={dfd}
        title="Data-flow diagram"
        caption="Bold boxes are external entities, rounded boxes are processes, cylinders are data stores; arrows carry the labelled data. The dashed arrow from the gate is a condition: nothing is exported when it is closed. Processes and stores link to their sections."
      />
    </section>

    <section id="deployment" class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Deployment</h2>
      <Diagram
        id="deployment"
        svg={deploy}
        title="UML deployment diagram"
        caption="Each shaded box is an environment the pipeline runs in; three-dimensional boxes are where code executes, folded notes are the jobs that run there, cylinders are stores. Arrows are labelled with the interface used."
      />
    </section>

    <section id="daily-run" class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">A daily run</h2>
      <Diagram
        id="sequence"
        svg={sequence}
        title="UML sequence diagram"
        caption="Time runs downward. Solid arrows with filled heads are calls, dashed arrows are replies, open heads are messages that do not wait. The alt fragment runs only when the gate is open."
      />
    </section>

    <section id="value" class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">How board value is computed</h2>
      <Diagram
        id="activity"
        svg={activity}
        title="UML activity diagram"
        caption="From the per-season lines to the value and rest-of-season value on the board. Bars fork and join work that runs independently: production per minute and minutes per game meet at the projection, and the games share joins at the value. Diamonds are decisions. Settings are read from the project, so the numbers here are the ones in use."
      />
    </section>

    <div id="stages" class="mt-10 grid max-w-3xl grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)] gap-8 text-sm leading-relaxed" data-testid="pipeline-stages">
      <section id="sources">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">1. Sources</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The league stats API (stats.wnba.com, stats.nba.com) is the system of record. Eight endpoints are fetched: the
          season schedule, the player directory, the team game log, the player game logs (the advanced line, once per
          season), traditional box scores, play-by-play, shot charts and team rosters. A ninth landing folder,
          advanced box scores per game, holds files from before the game logs replaced it and is still read.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          ESPN's public site API has five: scoreboards (what games are matched against), game summaries (a second box score
          for reconciliation), team and roster directories, and the injury report. Summaries also carry plays and team
          lines, which are landed but not used yet.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="ingest">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">2. Ingest</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The Ingest workflow runs daily at 09:15 UTC over the seven days ending that day, and on demand for any league,
          source or date range. ESPN jobs run on GitHub-hosted runners, one per league in parallel. The stats hosts refuse
          cloud runner addresses, so stats jobs run on two self-hosted runner containers at home, and only when the
          repository variable naming that runner is set (ADR 0002). One stats job per league runs at a time, shared with the
          Backfill workflow, which fetches whole seasons one season and season type at a time.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>Pacing.</strong> Every request goes through one paced client. The gap between requests starts at 2.25 s,
            with ±30% jitter, and adapts: ten clean, fast answers in a row shrink it by a tenth, an answer slower than 3 s
            widens it by a fifth, an error, timeout or retryable status by half, a 429 doubles it and adds the server's
            Retry-After and a 30 s pause. Daily runs keep the gap between 1.5 and 3 s with a 30 s read timeout; backfills may
            go down to 0.75 s with a 20 s timeout. Up to five retries back off from 1 to 60 s, and three timeouts in a row
            open a circuit that ends the run.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cookies.</strong> The client clears its cookies before every attempt: the stats hosts' bot-manager
            cookies made a long-lived session's requests hang until they timed out (ADR 0004).
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Request economy (ADR 0004).</strong> The advanced line comes from the player game logs once per season
            instead of per game. Backfills fetch shot charts per team and season; daily runs fetch one per game.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Idempotent.</strong> A payload already landed that day is not requested again, and one whose content
            hash matches its last landing is not written again; a full refresh bypasses both.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Failures.</strong> Only a missing ESPN game summary (404) counts as missing at source. Any other failed
            request fails the job, which opens an incident issue for that job; the day's build still runs with what
            landed, and the next daily window fetches the missing payloads again.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="landing">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">3. Landing</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Successful responses are stored as fetched, as JSON in the Unity Catalog volume
          <code>raw/landing/&lt;source&gt;/&lt;endpoint&gt;/&lt;league&gt;/&lt;season&gt;/&lt;date&gt;/</code>, each with a
          <code>.meta.json</code> sidecar (URL, parameters, status, size, SHA-256, fetch time) and an <code>_index.json</code>
          per scope. Every run also writes a run log under <code>landing/_ops/ingest_log/</code>. Nothing is transformed on
          the way in, so every dbt model can be rebuilt from what landed; the gate's history and the seeds are the
          exceptions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="bronze">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">4. Bronze</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The Build workflow starts when an Ingest or Backfill run finishes, even with a failed job (not when it is
          cancelled), on a GitHub-hosted runner, one build at a time:
          it makes sure every landing folder exists, then runs dbt on the Databricks SQL warehouse. The same project runs on
          DuckDB in CI over a small sample; engine differences (JSON access, file listing, hashing, dates, fuzzy matching)
          live in adapter-dispatched macros, and the fuzzy matching thresholds differ by engine.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          Bronze has {count('bronze')} incremental models, appending rows: one per endpoint, three for ESPN's game summary
          (players, plays, teams). Each run reads files landed on or after its newest landing date that it has not loaded,
          and every row keeps its source file, landing date, request key and payload hash.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="silver">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">5. Silver</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          {count('silver')} tables: typed and deduplicated (the latest landing wins), keys tested unique, the game type read
          from the game id, and a neutral-site game that both teams list as home folded into one.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>Crosswalks (ADR 0005).</strong> <a href={m('team_xwalk')} class={link}>Teams</a> match ESPN to the league
            by full name, then nickname from the team seed, then abbreviation.
            <a href={m('game_xwalk')} class={link}>Games</a> match on the same two teams and the same Eastern date (the next
            or previous day when none), each ESPN game used once. <a href={m('player_xwalk')} class={link}>Players</a> match by
            the override seed first; then by exact name (either order, nickname, first and last) on the same team in a
            matched game; then by fuzzy name similarity, where a matching birth date lowers the bar; keeping mutual best
            matches. The rest wait in <a href={m('player_xwalk_review')} class={link}>player_xwalk_review</a>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Reconciliation.</strong> <a href={m('player_game_reconciliation')} class={link}>Each player-game</a> gets
            one verdict across twelve counts and minutes: exact (counts agree, minutes within half a minute, or neither
            source has the player playing), minor (counts agree, minutes within one), or conflict. Each differing value is a
            row in <a href={m('reconciliation')} class={link}>ops.reconciliation</a>, and the league's figure wins unless it
            is missing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Lineups.</strong> <a href={m('event_lineups')} class={link}>event_lineups</a> puts five players a side
            on the floor for every play-by-play action. Substitutions name only the player leaving; the one coming on is
            matched to the box score by five ranked name rules (given-name prefixes, suffixes, changed names, reversed
            names, the last word of a longer name). Events are ordered by game clock, period starters come from the box
            score in the first period and are inferred after it.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="gold">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">6. Gold</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          {count('gold')} models. The star schema: <a href={m('dim_player')} class={link}>players</a> with team history as
          stints from where they actually played, teams, games and dates; facts per player-game, team-game, play-by-play
          action with lineups, shot (those two are incremental merges) and injury listing. The marts: category z-scores
          per window, rolling averages, the projection, the board, streamers and the upcoming schedule.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
          <li>
            <strong>Category z-scores</strong> (<a href={m('mart_category_zscores')} class={link}>mart_category_zscores</a>)
            for each season and the last 10 and 20 games, against the 180 NBA or 90 WNBA players with the most minutes in the
            window: per-game counting stats, turnovers negative, percentages volume-weighted as (player % − pool %) ×
            attempts per game.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Projection and value</strong>, as the activity diagram above shows: production per minute (minutes ×
            {f.fantasy_recency.join(', ')} by season), minutes per game (games × {nba.minutes_recency.join(', ')} in the NBA,
            {wnba.minutes_recency.join(', ')} in the WNBA; a past season spent on another team counts
            {f.fantasy_team_change} of its minutes, since the role was another team's, while per-minute production carries
            over; in season, with five or more games for his current team, blended with his last five games
            at {f.fantasy_recent_minutes}, so a role change shows at once), {nba.prior_games} or {wnba.prior_games} games of a
            replacement-level player, two scoring passes over a {nba.teams}×{nba.roster_spots} or {wnba.teams}×{wnba.roster_spots}
            league's pool, replacement level subtracted, then value above replacement times the games share (capped at 1).
            Values below replacement are never scaled.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Availability.</strong> ESPN's injury report is linked to players by name within the team. Out with no
            return date removes all remaining games; doubtful counts 0.75 of a game, questionable 0.5, day-to-day 0.25. Once
            the regular season is over, rest of season is the value itself.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Streamers</strong> (<a href={m('mart_streamers')} class={link}>mart_streamers</a>) compare each current
            player's last five games (minutes) and last ten (value) with their earlier games blended with their latest
            earlier season, and carry the board rank and the schedule ahead. Each upcoming game
            (<a href={m('mart_schedule_upcoming')} class={link}>mart_schedule_upcoming</a>) has a small matchup value for
            each side, from the opponent's defensive rating and the expected pace, both blending this season to date with
            last season (last season counting as {f.fantasy_matchup.prior_games} games) and standardised over the league.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Settings chosen by backtest</strong> (ADR 0003): WNBA 2021 to 2026 and NBA 2024-25 and 2025-26, replayed
            before each season and a twentieth, a tenth, a quarter and half way in. Tested and left out because they did
            not predict better: counting the current season for more early on, dropping games cut short (often by an
            injury), and a player's past results against a given opponent.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="ops">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">7. Ops</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          {count('ops')} dbt models: the ingest run log (read straight from the landing run logs), every reconciliation
          difference, and match and conflict rates per league and season (printed in every build log). Two more tables are
          written by the gate script, not dbt: <code>test_results</code> and <code>publish_gate</code>. Data Health shows
          the gate, the checks, the latest 100 ingest runs and the reconciliation rates; the individual differences are in
          the SQL console and the reconciliation notebook.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="gate">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">8. Quality gate</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          dbt builds and tests together, layer by layer, so a failing test skips the models downstream of it. There are
          {schema.generic_tests} column tests (keys, not null, relationships, accepted values), all blocking, and
          {checks.length} <a href={href('/schema/#checks')} class={link}>data-quality checks</a>: {errorChecks.length} blocking
          (completeness against the schedule, in-season freshness, box-score arithmetic and closure, rostered players and
          sane values on the board, and a chaos check that fails only in a drill) and {warnChecks.length} warnings (volume
          anomalies, crosswalk coverage, injury links, lineup completeness, event and box agreement, minutes closure, shot
          locations, team turnovers).
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The gate script records every model and test result and closes the gate on any blocking failure or model error;
          then nothing is exported. The incident issue covers every step of the build, from dbt to the export, promotion,
          notebooks, extract and sheet push: it names the steps that failed, carries the end of the job log with sample
          failing rows, is commented on while it recurs and closes itself only when every step that ran succeeded. A
          missed game blocks the gate only once it has been final for more than a day, so one failed request does not. CI runs the column tests on every change;
          the data-quality checks need real data and run only in the nightly build.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="export">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">9. Export (ADR 0006)</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          A passing build is read from the warehouse as Arrow and written to R2 as <code>builds/&lt;id&gt;/</code>: Parquet
          for every gold and ops table and the category seed (the big facts split per league and season); compact JSON for
          the board, streamers and schedule, rounded to four decimals; JSON for the dimensions, injuries, reconciliation
          rates, run log and gate; one file per player; <code>health.json</code>; a manifest with each file's rows and
          SHA-256. The build is staged: nothing points at it while the notebooks and the Tableau extract read it by id and
          add their files. Then it is promoted: <code>builds.json</code>, then <code>latest.json</code>, so the live build
          is always complete; seven builds are kept. A rollback (Export workflow) points <code>latest.json</code> at an
          earlier build and pins it: nightly builds are still recorded but do not go live until the pin is released. A
          build exported by hand checks the latest publish gate first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="serving">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">10. Serving</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          A Cloudflare Worker serves this static Astro site and, under <code>/data/</code>, the R2 bucket with range
          requests. The pointers (<code>latest.json</code>, <code>builds.json</code>) are cached for a minute and fetched
          without the browser cache; every build file is cached for a year as immutable. Player pages
          <code>/p/&lt;league&gt;/&lt;id&gt;/</code> are rewritten to one page. The content security policy allows inline
          code only by hash, WebAssembly for DuckDB, DuckDB's extension host and the Tableau embed; the dbt docs and the
          notebook pages have their own policies. Pages read the build in the browser; the Schema and Pipeline pages are
          built from the project instead. The SQL console loads DuckDB-WASM from R2 and reads the build's Parquet by range; the Parquet tables are private to the site's owner (ADR 0008), so the console asks the owner to sign in with Yahoo, and the notebooks and the Tableau extract read with a key CI derives from the Yahoo client secret.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          The site deploys when its code changes, never when data does: production from <code>main</code>, a preview Worker
          from <code>preprod</code> reading the same bucket. Every change runs page tests on desktop and phone, accessibility
          checks and a phone-width check against fixture data; every deploy runs Lighthouse three times on the home page and
          holds the median LCP under 2 s.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="analysis">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">11. Analysis</h2>
        <p class="mt-2">
          After a passing nightly build, five <a href={href('/notebooks/')} class={link}>notebooks</a> (a backtest of the
          board, the streamer signals, game context, a data quality scorecard and shooting) run against the staged build over HTTPS and add their pages and findings
          to it before it goes live; if they fail, the build goes live without them and the incident says so. The Tableau extract does the same: three CSVs into the build and into a Google Sheet, which
          Tableau Public re-reads about daily for the <a href={href('/dashboards/')} class={link}>dashboard</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section id="workflows" class="mt-10 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Other workflows</h2>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li><strong>Backfill:</strong> whole seasons per league, serially, on the same runners and client as Ingest.</li>
        <li><strong>Export data:</strong> build (after checking the latest gate), roll back (pinning), unpin, or list.</li>
        <li><strong>Query the lakehouse:</strong> read-only; prints any table, filtered and ordered, into a job log.</li>
        <li><strong>Probe sources:</strong> checks from a GitHub runner that the stats API, ESPN and Databricks answer.</li>
        <li><strong>CI:</strong> secret scan; ruff, mypy, pytest; the dbt sample built twice (the second run takes the incremental path) with the gate; the schema file's freshness; notebooks and the extract on the sample; page tests; Worker tests and a dry-run deploy.</li>
        <li><strong>Databricks job:</strong> every build also deploys a scheduled Databricks job of the same dbt project, kept paused.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="gaps" class="mt-10 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed" data-testid="known-gaps">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight">Gaps found and closed</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-muted">Found by checking this page against the code (issue #96), each closed since:</p>
      <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
        <li>One failed request skipped the day's build: the build now runs after any ingest that finishes.</li>
        <li>A build exported by hand skipped the gate: the Export workflow checks the latest gate first.</li>
        <li>Notebooks and the extract were added to a live build: builds are staged, completed, then promoted.</li>
        <li>A failure after the gate opened no incident: the incident covers every step and names the failures.</li>
        <li>The next nightly build undid a rollback: a rollback pins the site until it is released.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </section>
</Base>
